Let $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ be consecutive terms of an arithmetic progression such that $b-a = c-b = d$ for some common difference $d.$ i.e., $a = a,$ $b = a+d,$ and $c=a+2d.$ If $a,$ $c,$ and $b$ are consecutive terms of a geometric progression, then $ac = b^2,$ which results in
\begin{align*} a(a+2d) & = (a+d)^2\\ a^2+2ad & = a^2+2ad+d^2\\ d^2 & = 0. \end{align*}
Thus, $d=0,$ and therefore, $a=b=c.$ So the smallest possible value of $c$ that satisfies the condition must be $\boxed{-100000},$ i.e., the smallest possible value of $a,$ which is also the smallest possible value of $c.$ </think> <answer> -100000 </answer>

